---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const dueHref = toolPathBySlug('pregnancy-due-date-calculator');
const diffHref = toolPathBySlug('date-difference-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>When am I ovulating? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Subtract the luteal phase from the cycle length, then add that many days to the first
      day of the last period. That is what a luteal phase counts. A 28 day cycle and a 14 day
      luteal phase means 14 days after the last period. For example, a last period on
      1 January 2026 is ovulation on 15 January 2026. The fertile window is the five days
      before that date through the ovulation day, so 10 January 2026 to 15 January 2026.
      Count the same period on 10 January and the page says you are inside the fertile window.
    </p>
    <p>
      Change the luteal phase and the ovulation date and the fertile window move together.
      For example, a 12 day luteal phase from that same January day is ovulation on
      17 January 2026, two days later, and the window becomes 12 January 2026 through
      17 January 2026. Seeing how a 12 day luteal phase moves 15 January 2026 to
      17 January 2026 is the whole point of the note. The next period stays 29 January 2026,
      because the next period is one cycle length after the last period, not a luteal
      calculation. At 14 the note stays hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chat reply can recite "day 14." It will not keep the last period, the cycle length,
      the luteal phase, and the window on one screen while you edit one of them. The
      arithmetic stays in the browser. Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Ovulation Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="luteal">
    <h2>How does the luteal phase move the date? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The luteal phase is the span from ovulation to the next period. What a luteal phase
      counts, on this page, is that span in whole days. A 14 day phase is the usual count.
      You adjust ovulation for a luteal phase that is not 14 days by typing the length you
      mean. Each day below 14 adds one day to the wait after the last period. Each day above
      14 subtracts one. A shorter phase does not move ovulation earlier. It moves it later,
      because the next period stays put and the luteal span is the part just before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The field accepts 10 to 16 days. For example, a 16 day luteal phase from 1 January
      2026 puts ovulation on 13 January 2026, two days earlier, and the note says two days
      earlier. A 10 day phase would move the date four days later, to 19 January 2026. The
      fertile window slides with ovulation. The next period does not. A 14 day luteal phase
      versus a 12 day luteal phase is 15 January against 17 January, with 29 January unchanged.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cycle length is the other number. It accepts 21 to 45 days. For example, a 32 day cycle
      and a 14 day luteal phase from 1 January 2026 put ovulation on 19 January 2026, and the
      next period on 2 February 2026. That 19 January date is the same estimate a conception
      date from the last period gets on the due-date page when the cycle is 32 and the luteal
      assumption is 14. The two pages agree on purpose when the luteal phase is 14.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The next period stays on the cycle length">
      <p>
        The next period stays on the cycle length. A shorter luteal phase does not make the
        cycle shorter. It places ovulation later inside the same cycle, by the number of days
        the phase differs from 14.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="window">
    <h2>How is a fertile window dated? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      How a fertile window is dated here is five days before ovulation through the ovulation
      day. That is six days, not the whole cycle. The fertile window versus ovulation date is
      a span against one day: the span is when sperm from an earlier day can still meet the
      egg, and the single day is the estimate of when the egg is present. 1 January 2026 with
      a 14 day phase dates the window 10 January through 15 January. The same period with a
      12 day phase dates it 12 January through 17 January.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leave "Count on" blank and the page uses today. Fill it when you want a past or future
      day. The last period has to be on or before that date. A later period is rejected,
      because a window measured backwards from a swapped pair of dates is not a cycle.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the first day of the window the status says you are inside it. On 10 January 2026
      that is the 14 day example. One day after ovulation, 16 January 2026, the page says
      1 day after the window. Past the window is allowed. The date you type is the date the
      status uses. Checking a fertile window before using an ovulation test is a reason to
      open the page. The test is still the confirmation. This count is not the test.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Today is a date, not a guess">
      <p>
        Blank means today on this device. A worked example pins both dates so the status
        cannot change overnight. 1 January 2026 counted on 10 January 2026 is inside the
        window when the luteal phase is 14, and two days before it when the phase is 12.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="period">
    <h2>When does the next period start? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      When the next period starts is one cycle length after the first day of the last period.
      A 28 day cycle from 1 January 2026 starts the next period on 29 January 2026. The next
      period on 29 January 2026 stays put when the luteal phase changes. A 12 day phase and a
      16 day phase both still say 29 January. If you expected the period to slide with
      ovulation, the card is telling you the page did not do that.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 32 day cycle starts the next period on 2 February 2026. February 2026 is not a leap
      month, and the page does not need one: 1 January plus 32 days is 2 February either way.
      The ovulation date for that longer cycle, with a 14 day phase, is 19 January. The window
      is 14 January to 19 January. Cycle length moved both the window and the period. Luteal
      length would have moved only the window.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is one cycle. The page does not keep a log of earlier periods, and it does not
      average several cycles. You type the length you mean. A fertile window calculator that
      stored a year of periods would be a different page. This one recomputes the dates you
      can see.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Which mistakes move the date? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving a shorter luteal phase at 14">
      <p>
        A 12 day luteal phase counted as 14 lands two days early: 15 January instead of
        17 January in the January 2026 example. The note appears only after you type 12. If
        the field still says 14, the page is doing what you asked, and the date is the early
        one. That is the failure a hidden 14 day assumption produces.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting the next period to move too">
      <p>
        The next period is the cycle length. Adjusting ovulation does not rewind or advance
        the period. 29 January is the next period at luteal phase 14 and at luteal phase 12.
        Read the ovulation card if you wanted the date that actually moved.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Typing the ovulation date as the last period">
      <p>
        The first field is the first day of bleeding, not the day you hope to ovulate. Put
        15 January 2026 in that field and the page will project a window about 14 days after
        January, which is a different cycle. The tool cannot tell those two dates apart. You can.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="This is not an ovulation test">
      <p>
        No strip, no temperature chart, no pregnancy prediction. If a test has already
        marked a different day, that day is the one to plan around. This page is the
        arithmetic you can rerun when the luteal phase was wrong. To estimate a conception
        date from the last period, read the ovulation date. It is an estimate, and a
        conception date calculator that claims more than that is overselling the count.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An ovulation calculator versus the alternatives keeps the luteal phase on the page and
      recomputes the ovulation date, the fertile window, and the next period together. A due
      date from the same last period belongs in the
      <a href={dueHref}>pregnancy due date calculator</a>. When the luteal phase is 14, that
      page's conception estimate matches this ovulation date. A span of calendar days that is
      not a cycle belongs in the <a href={diffHref}>date difference calculator</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Come back when the count date changes, or when you learn the luteal phase is not 14.
      The same last period and the same cycle produce a new ovulation date in one edit. Leave
      the luteal phase at 14 only when 14 is the phase you mean. A different length should be
      visible next to the ovulation date, which is what the note is for.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Check 1 January 2026 →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
